{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "265:\subsection*{Limitations}
266-
267-\paragraph{Single benchmark and backbone.} All experiments were conducted on LIBERO/SafeLIBERO with $\pi_{0.5}$. Validating on additional benchmarks (ManiSkill, RoboCasa) and VLA architectures (Octo, RT-2) would strengthen generality claims.
268-
269-\paragraph{Known geometry assumption.} Ground-truth SDF labels require analytical scene geometry (MuJoCo). Real-world deployment will need neural SDF estimation from depth or point clouds, and the effect of label noise on collision shaping remains uncharacterized.
270-
271-\paragraph{Simulation only.} All results are simulated; a real-robot validation is needed to confirm that internalized collision awareness survives the sim-to-real gap.
272-
273-\paragraph{Binary collision definition.} SafeLIBERO defines collision as binary contact (any contact constitutes a collision). For tasks requiring push-through-obstacle motions or compliant contact, the collision penalty may over-suppress necessary interactions---a general limitation of collision-free safety paradigms. Future work could address this via task-conditioned safety margins or force-aware collision thresholds.
